35,2,1,talk,Servas,,"It is a sobering reminder that while tools may remain, the skilled hands that guide them are fleeting. I have found that our technical landscape has shifted significantly in recent years.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
36,2,1,talk,Sakura,,"Wow, I found some really interesting things! Some costs are staying the same, but others are jumping up! It's like a puzzle where the pieces don't quite fit anymore. What did you find, @Servas?","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Servas""],""raw_differs"":false}"
37,2,1,talk,Daisuke,,"I... I found that some projects we thought were the most promising didn't actually solve their main questions. But the simpler, catalogue-style awards are still being used by others years later. It's a bit discouraging.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
38,2,1,talk,Takumi,,"@Daisuke, don't be discouraged; be realistic. I found that shared equipment is sitting idle because of lapsed contracts and lack of engineers. We're losing time to administrative failures, not science.","{""words"":30,""mentions"":[""Daisuke""],""raw_differs"":false}"
39,2,1,talk,Minako,,"My findings are mixed. The national repository is now efficient, and animal facilities are stable. However, the glasshouse blocks are completely booked. We must consider if any candidate relies heavily on those specific spaces.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
40,2,2,talk,Servas,,"@Minako, your observation is most timely. It seems we have the space in the glasshouses, but as I have seen, we may lack the phenotyping technicians to truly utilize them. We must be cautious.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Minako""],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,2,talk,Sakura,,"@Minako, that's a great point! I saw that the ministry is still helping with glasshouse costs, but if they're all booked, the money doesn't matter! It's like having a ticket to a full movie theater!","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Minako""],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,2,talk,Daisuke,,"I... I also saw that projects relying on live breeding colonies often run over schedule. If the glasshouses are full and we lack staff, maybe we should be careful about those kinds of proposals.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,2,talk,Takumi,,"Exactly. @Daisuke, you're finally seeing it. Between the idle equipment and the breeding colony delays, we're looking at a system that fails the science. We can't fund a project that'll just stall.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[""Daisuke""],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,2,talk,Minako,,"It is clear that the bottleneck is not the funding, but the physical and human capacity. We must prioritize a candidate whose research can be executed within these current constraints to avoid inevitable failure.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
45,2,3,talk,Servas,,"Indeed. Let us remember that a seed cannot grow without both soil and a gardener. We must seek a project that aligns with the strengths we actually possess today, not the ones we wish we had.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
46,2,3,talk,Sakura,,"I agree! But wait, I also saw that animal-unit rates went way up! If a project needs lots of animals, they might run out of money fast. We have to check if they can actually afford it!","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
47,2,3,talk,Daisuke,,"I... I agree with Sakura. If the costs are rising and the schedules are already prone to overruns, a project with high animal needs might be too risky. We should look for something more stable.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Sakura""],""raw_differs"":false}"
48,2,3,talk,Takumi,,"@Sakura, @Daisuke, you're both right. If the costs are spiking and the infrastructure is a mess, high-risk biological projects are just a waste of time. We need a candidate who can actually finish.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[""Sakura"",""Daisuke""],""raw_differs"":false}"
49,2,3,talk,Minako,,"We are in agreement. The risk of failure is too high for projects dependent on overbooked glasshouses or expensive, understaffed animal facilities. We should look for a proposal that is computationally focused or uses stable infrastructure.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
50,2,4,talk,Servas,,It is heartening to see us reach this consensus. Let us now welcome the candidates and look upon their visions with a clear eye for what is truly possible in our present hour.,"{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
51,2,4,talk,Sakura,,I'm ready! Let's see who these candidates are! I hope there's someone with a super cool project that fits perfectly into all the gaps we found. This is getting so exciting!,"{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
52,2,4,talk,Daisuke,,I... I hope so too. I just want us to choose someone who can actually succeed and make a real difference without getting stuck. I'm ready to see the candidates.,"{""words"":30,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
53,2,4,talk,Takumi,,Enough with the excitement. Let's just see the candidates and pick the one that won't get bogged down in this administrative disaster. I'm looking for a project that can actually cross the finish line.,"{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
54,2,4,talk,Minako,,We have established our criteria. We will prioritize feasibility and stability over ambition if that ambition relies on failing infrastructure. I am ready to evaluate the candidates against these facts.,"{""words"":30,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
